@meta
id: 10-16
title: Arrays and angle brackets
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-brace, obj-paren
par: 79
minutes: 6
vscode: select between the brackets and count on your eyes
open: order.repository.ts

@brief
The delimiter objects are a family, not three special cases. Every one of
them works the same way:

    i( a(    round brackets — calls and parameter lists
    i{ a{    curly braces — objects and blocks
    i[ a[    square brackets — arrays and index expressions
    i< a<    angle brackets — generics and JSX props

Once that clicks there is nothing left to learn about them. The only
question you ever ask is which delimiter, and the answer is on the screen
in front of you.

Two jobs in this file. A 500 from the payment provider means the request
was wrong, so retrying it just fails again slower — the retry list should
be `[502, 503, 504, 429]`. And `LegacyOrderRecord` was renamed to
`OrderRecord` when the legacy tables were dropped, so both type arguments
need updating.

@keys
| `ci[` | change what is inside the square brackets |
| `ci<` | change what is inside the angle brackets |
| `a[` `a<` | with the brackets |
| `i]` `i>` | the same objects, closing-bracket spelling |

@hints
Three edits, three different kinds of bracket, one idea.
--
`ci[` for the array, `ci<` for each of the two type arguments.
--
`f[` and `f<` get you onto the bracket, which counts as inside it. Then
the object, then type.

@solution
    f[ ci[ 502, 503, 504, 429 <Esc>
    3j 0 f< ci< OrderRecord <Esc>
    j 0 f< ci< OrderRecord <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Sixty-seven keystrokes, forty-two of them typed content.

The angle-bracket pair is the one people forget exists, and it is the one
that pays off most in TypeScript. A generic argument is exactly the kind
of thing that is annoying to select: it is buried in the middle of a long
declaration, it contains commas, and the closing bracket is easy to
mistake for a comparison. `ci<` does not care.

It works in JSX too, where `i<` is the contents of the opening tag —
useful when a component's props all change at once. Do not confuse it
with the tag object `it`, which is the next lesson and means something
quite different.

@start order.repository.ts
export const RETRYABLE_CODES = [500, 502, 503, 504];

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<LegacyOrderRecord> {
  public async findMany(where: Partial<LegacyOrderRecord>) {
    return this.model.findMany({ where });
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
export const RETRYABLE_CODES = [502, 503, 504, 429];

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord> {
  public async findMany(where: Partial<OrderRecord>) {
    return this.model.findMany({ where });
  }
}
